    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-29-burnham-to-fight-next-election-on-pledge-to-change-voting-sy",
      "title": "Burnham to fight next election on pledge to change voting system",
      "summary": "The prime minister says he wants other parties to take part in a national commission on electoral reform.",
      "body": "Andy Burnham has promised that Labour will fight the next general election on a pledge to change the UK's voting system.In his first party conference speech as prime minister, he announced plans for a national commission to explore options for electoral reform.He invited other parties to work with the commission to ensure \"everyone's vote counts, and everyone's voice is heard\".He has previously supported moves towards a proportional representation (PR) model, telling last year's conference that \"PR's time has come\".Westminster's first-past-the-post system, under which candidates with the most votes in each seat are elected, has been called into question as the UK political landscape has fragmented in recent years.Debate over the system has intensified since Labour amassed a huge parliamentary majority with a 34% vote share in 2024.Around 100 Labour MPs recently supported a parliamentary amendment in favour of setting up a commission to examine alternative options, a move Burnham himself supported last year when mayor of Greater Manchester.In his speech Burnham told delegates: \"We will all have failed in our public duties if we carry on as we are and open the door of No 10 to a divisive government on less 30% of the vote or less.\"He added: \"We can't succeed in the eyes of the world if we continue with the levels of political turbulence we have seen in recent times.\"Burnham did not set out details of who would lead his proposed commission, or when it would begin its work.Any move to change the voting system would be supported by the Liberal Democrats, who had previously urged Burnham to introduce proportional representation before the next election, which must be held by 2029.However, any move away from first-past-the-post is likely to be resisted by the Conservatives. Reform UK backed introducing PR at the last election, but there has been speculation that its enthusiasm for the move may have since cooled.Support for changing the voting system is also not universal within Labour, with deputy leader Lucy Powell acknowledging after Burnham's speech that \"parts of the Labour Party\" are not in favour of it.Speaking to Politics Live on BBC 2, she said she personally backed electoral reform but declined to specify a proposed new system, adding there were \"lots of models\" available.At the last UK election the Liberal Democrats backed the introduction of a single transferable vote (STV) system, which is used for Scottish council elections.Other options include the additional member system, a hybrid model that combines first-past-the-post with regional party lists that is used to elect members of the Scottish Parliament.Earlier this year, the Welsh Senedd was elected for the first time using a closed-list proportional representation system.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-29T15:55:34.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/e36a/live/dff20db0-bc1a-11f1-b84f-b5367b64d530.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm158en2qxg9o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-29-sir-ranulph-fiennes-relatives-tell-bbc-it-s-very-painful-not",
      "title": "Sir Ranulph Fiennes' relatives tell BBC it's 'very painful' not being able to visit him",
      "summary": "It has been alleged that Sir Ranulph has been admitted to a number of care homes under assumed names.",
      "body": "Relatives of the British explorer Sir Ranulph Fiennes have said it is \"very painful\" he appears to have been hidden away from them. Speaking to BBC Newsnight, his former sister-in-law told the BBC that she did not know where he was, and nobody had been able to see him.It has been alleged that Sir Ranulph, 82, has been admitted to a number of care homes under assumed names, after a family rift involving his second wife, Louise Millington-Cotes.Louise Millington-Cotes did not immediately respond to the allegations, or the BBC's request for comment.Cheshire Police have said they are aware of the concerns regarding Sir Ranulph, and are satisfied he is living in an appropriate setting with suitable care.They said there were \"no immediate safeguarding concerns\". Sir Ranulph has not been seen in public for nearly two years, just months after opening up about a diagnosis of Parkinson's disease, a progressive disorder affecting the brain, nervous system, and muscle control.He was the first person to visit both north and south poles by surface means in the early 1980s and has been described by Guinness World Records as \"the world's greatest living explorer\".Spending his childhood between the UK and South Africa, he joined the British Army after leaving school, going on to serve in the SAS.Sir Ranulph's stepson, Alex Millington-Cotes, told BBC Newsnight he wanted to see the power of attorney \"removed from my own mother\" and placed in the hands of people who \"have fully his best interests in mind\".\"He's not an isolated type of person, and so to see him isolated now is very painful,\" he said. Last week, he launched a crowdfunding campaign for legal fees to \"locate and safeguard\" the explorer, and so far it has raised almost £26,000. He wrote that the fundraiser had been created as \"a result of our concern and desperation\" after trying \"multiple avenues\" to locate his stepfather.Questions about Sir Ranulph's whereabouts grew in prominence after a report in the Telegraph earlier this month, external in which \"members of his inner circle\" raised concerns about his whereabouts. The paper also reported concerns about Sir Ranulph have been raised with the Office of the Public Guardian (OPG), the government agency which helps people to stay in control of decisions about their health and finances. In a statement to the BBC, an OPG spokesperson said: \"We have a strict duty of confidentiality to all individuals who have made a lasting power of attorney, and it would be deeply inappropriate to comment on a specific case.\"We take all safeguarding concerns seriously and investigate thoroughly where there is the evidence and legal basis to do so.\"Arabella Pepper, the younger sister of Sir Ranulph's late first wife Ginny, told BBC Newsnight that she had not been able to see her former brother-in-law for over a year. \"All his friends and family want to see him and take him Toblerones and cheer him up, but we don't know where is, and he's not available, he's not accessible - that's what worries us,\" she said.She alleges that she was only able to see him in July 2025 after she admitted herself to a care home where she had been told he was staying. \"The first thing I said to him, apart from the usual greetings, was that everybody was worried about him, was looking for him, was longing to see him,\" Pepper told the BBC.\"The relief on his face was very obvious, and he said, send a message to everybody, send them my love, tell them I love them and tell them I'm longing to see them.\"Posting on X, BBC world affairs editor John Simpson, wrote he was \"horrified to hear what's happened to my good friend Ran Fiennes\".\"He's the world's bravest man, and he's vulnerable. We must know what's happening to him.\"Starting in 1967, Sir Ranulph has been the leader of almost 20 major expeditions, according to the Explorers Club.His expedition to both the north and south poles came during the Transglobe Expedition, in which he led the first team to circumnavigate the globe from north to south.Sir Ranulph has also enjoyed a career as a prolific travel writer, as well as authoring fiction, non-fiction and biographies.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-29T15:52:32.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/671f/live/1384a860-bbe2-11f1-bd21-bdf910f2cec6.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqy7zr6d2lj4o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-29-watch-how-did-burnham-s-speech-go-down-in-the-conference-hal",
      "title": "Watch: How did Burnham's speech go down in the conference hall?",
      "summary": "The PM received a standing ovation as he spoke about his late dad while setting out reforms to social care.",
      "body": "Andy Burnham has said the government will end the pensions \"triple lock\" in an emotional speech at his first Labour conference as prime minister. Reflecting on the recent death of his father, Burnham set how he was determined to honour his memory by creating a new national care service funded by savings from pensions from 2030.He also announced a plan to end the ban on water companies being owned publicly and a proposal to change the UK's electoral system. The BBC's Jack Fenwick explains how the big announcements were received by Labour MPs and members in Liverpool. More on this story.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-29T15:31:27.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/b2d0/live/20851440-bc1a-11f1-bd21-bdf910f2cec6.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cwz0zk9d1497o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-29-burnham-says-public-body-will-invest-in-electricity-grid",
      "title": "Burnham says public body will invest in electricity grid",
      "summary": "The prime minister said he wants to reduce energy costs in his first conference speech as Labour leader.",
      "body": "Andy Burnham has announced a new government body to invest in Britain's electricity grid in his first party conference speech as Labour leader.The prime minister told party activists in Liverpool that the new publicly owned company, branded Great British Grid, will speed up connections to the grid by driving up competition for connection projects.Burnham is putting the proposals at the heart of a new goal to bring UK energy costs in line with other nations in Europe within 10 years.He promised on Tuesday to have an \"honest conversation\" with the public and take on the \"difficult issues\" that have been ignored for too long.Burnham put greater public control of utilities at the centre of his pitch for power over the summer.\"A publicly-owned company that will challenge the private sector operators,\" he told the Labour conference.\"It will increase competition, drive down costs and speed up connections.\"So businesses can expand and grow more quickly.\"There are a number of reasons for the high energy prices in the UK, part of which comes from state subsidies given to private companies to modernise the grid.This is to accommodate new wind and solar farms and transmit their power to homes and businesses - a cost which ends up being added to the bills of customers.Full details of the new body are yet to be set out, but Labour said it would invest in and compete for transmission connection projects alongside the three private companies that currently own and manage the grid in Britain.The party said it would also make it easier for firms waiting to be connected to the grid to finance connection infrastructure themselves, with GB Grid supporting and potentially co-investing in projects alongside them.Labour has said that funding for the new body will be found within the existing budget of GB Energy, the public green investment company set up by Labour last year and which is now headquartered in Aberdeen.The transition of electricity generation from large fossil fuel stations to the more remote locations used for renewables, such as offshore windfarms, will require Britain's ageing transmission grid to undergo extensive rewiring in coming years.Energy regulator Ofgem has estimated that £70bn of investment is required in the grid between 2025 and 2031, quadrupling the current rate of investment.But in a report earlier this month, the National Audit Office warned that the number of projects waiting to connect to the grid had grown \"significantly\" whilst delays to projects were also increasing.Conservative shadow energy secretary Andrew Bowie said the prime minister wanted to \"nationalise the network\", adding: \"The Conservatives would get out of the way, letting businesses build their own gas power and grid connections and bid openly for capacity, and cutting electricity costs for every business through our Cheap Power Plan.\"Speaking to BBC Radio 4's Today programme, Matthew Lawrence, founder of the think tank Common Wealth and former adviser to Burnham, said the UK is \"unusual in that it has a wholly privatised grid\", which had led to \"decades of sluggishness development and chronic underinvestment\".He said Tuesday's announcement \"seems to have fixed that by bringing in public investment into new parts of the grid\".Dhara Vyas, chief executive of Energy UK, said the investment was welcome, but \"the bulk of this will be private investment\". \"In the next five years the transmission owners, the ones doing that highway of pylons, getting the electricity from source to homes to businesses, they are spending £90bn in the next five years\"In his speech, which centered on the theme of \"hope\", Burnham added that the UK will have a national energy plan for the first time and give stronger powers to mayors and local leaders.\"Energy, water, housing. Back under stronger public control,\" he told delegates.\"We rely on them, we pay for them and once again we will have power over them.\"He said he would be \"pragmatic\" in relation to North Sea oil and gas amid looming decisions on Jackdaw and Rosebank \"as we build a bridge to a clean energy future as we confront the climate crisis\".",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-29T15:14:39.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/6bac/live/19d68ae0-bbc8-11f1-bd21-bdf910f2cec6.jpg",
