      "id": "theverge-2026-09-28-honor-s-magic-9-pro-max-has-a-big-camera-and-a-bigger-batter",
      "title": "Honor’s Magic 9 Pro Max has a big camera and a bigger battery",
      "summary": "This mossy green looks great, but the 9 Pro Max is also launching in black and silver. | Image: Honor\t\n\nHonor launches its new Magic 9 flagship phones in China today, and the 9 Pro Max features a design revamp, a capable camera, and a colossal battery.\nThis is Honor's first flagship launch - Robot Phone aside - since it announced its collaboration with camera company Arri. The initial impact is tw",
      "body": "Honor launches its new Magic 9 flagship phones in China today, and the 9 Pro Max features a design revamp, a capable camera, and a colossal battery.\nThis is Honor’s first flagship launch — Robot Phone aside — since it announced its collaboration with camera company Arri. The initial impact is twofold. The new camera island is Arri-inspired, drawing from the “three-lens turret” design first introduced in 1937’s Arriflex 35, complete with a knurled texture around the main lens.\n[Image: Arri used variants of this three-lens turret design across most of its film cameras. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/20260909-143532.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nArri has played a part in the camera itself, contributing the ability to shoot in Arri LogC3, together with 14 preset Arri Look LUTs, access to Arri’s wider library of official and third-party LUTs, and support for the APV 10-bit 4:2:2 codec. The cameras themselves include a 200-megapixel main lens using a 1/1.28-inch-type sensor, a 3.5x telephoto using the same resolution and a 1/1.4-inch sensor, and a 50-megapixel ultrawide — though this latter lens doesn’t support all the Arri Cinema Mode features. A “cinema-grade” directional microphone should help with video recording too. Less technically impressive, but just as useful, will be the square-format selfie camera, which like Apple’s Center Stage will be able to capture in either landscape or portrait.\nHonor says that the wider camera island is more space-efficient, leaving extra space for a huge 8,800mAh battery, now up to 40 percent silicon content. The 9 Pro Max is powered by the Snapdragon 8 Elite Extreme Gen 6. I asked Honor why it picked the Extreme version, given the 9 Pro Max doesn’t support the 8K60 or 4K240 video recording that chip enables, and was told it was “about delivering a complete and consistent flagship experience, rather than pursuing a single benchmark result.”\n[Image: The Magic 9 Pro Max supports Honor’s G200 and G500 teleconverter lenses, plus a custom SmallRig video kit. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/honor-magic-9-pro-camera-accessories.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nThe Magic 9 series launches in China today. Honor has confirmed that an international launch is coming, but we’re still waiting to find out when.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Gadgets",
        "Mobile",
        "News",
        "Phones",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Dominic Preston",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-28T08:00:36.000Z",
