      "id": "bbc-2026-09-28-felled-sycamore-gap-stump-has-encouraging-growth",
      "title": "Felled Sycamore Gap stump has 'encouraging' growth",
      "summary": "A thick layer of growth surrounds the stump three years after the landmark was illegally felled.",
      "body": "Felled Sycamore Gap stump has 'encouraging' growthImage source, National Trust Images/PAImage caption, The tree stump on Hadrian's Wall has been surrounded by a fence to protect itThe stump of the iconic Sycamore Gap tree is showing \"encouraging\" signs with a thick ring of new shoots growing around it, three years on from its illegal felling.The landmark on Hadrian's Wall in Northumberland was targeted on 28 September 2023 by Daniel Graham and Adam Carruthers, who travelled during a storm from their homes in Cumbria to cut it down.Their actions sparked widespread outrage and the pair were later jailed for criminal damage to the Victorian-planted tree and Roman wall.Site owners the National Trust fenced off the stump to see if it would recover, with general manager Andrew Poad saying he is \"filled with hope by the way nature is taking its course\".The Sycamore Gap - From Roots to RuinA sycamore tree, growing in a natural dip along Hadrian’s Wall, came to symbolise a community. Chopped down overnight, it became a story that made headlines around the world. Who destroyed the tree at Sycamore Gap, and why?He said: \"These flourishing shoots are really encouraging and show the special measures we've taken, including reducing soil compaction around the roots and limiting human disturbance, are paying off.\"Poad said the trust was grateful to visitors supporting their efforts to give the tree \"the best possible chance to thrive\".He added: \"We're monitoring the tree closely and, over time, our rangers will manage it depending on how it develops.\"Image source, National Trust Images/PAImage caption, A thick layer of new shoots now surrounds the Sycamore Gap stumpAfter the tree was felled, seedlings were kept in a secret National Trust centre to help preserve the legacy of the much-photographed landmark.Saplings were then donated to community groups and charities around the country, with art projects launched to highlight nature's importance and condemn the illegal felling.Image source, Mark BeadleImage caption, The Sycamore Gap tree was a landmark of Northumberland and northern EnglandGraham and Carruthers were jailed for four years and three months in July 2025 for their roles in chopping down the tree with a chainsaw.The pair, who were 39 and 32 at the time of their conviction, filmed themselves in the act and took a wedge of trunk away as a trophy.Graham, a groundworker from near Carlisle, and Carruthers, a mechanic from Wigton, never explained the motive for what prosecutors called a \"moronic mission\", but sought to blame each other during the trial.Image caption, The site is looked after by the National Trust and Northumberland National ParkThe tree was planted in the late 1800s and became a much-loved visitor destination and landmark at the former frontier of the Roman Empire.It found global fame after featuring in a scene of the 1991 film Robin Hood Prince of Thieves, starring Kevin Costner, and was popular with photographers and artists.Follow BBC North East on X, external and Facebook, external and BBC Cumbria on X, external and Facebook, external and both on Nextdoor and Instagram, external. Get in touchDo you have a story suggestion for BBC North East & Cumbria?More on this storyRelated internet links",
