      "id": "bbc-2026-09-27-two-mass-shootings-in-south-africa-leave-27-dead",
      "title": "Two mass shootings in South Africa leave 27 dead",
      "summary": "The two attacks hours apart come as the country struggles to stop high levels of gang-related armed violence.",
      "body": "Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Gunmen torched vehicles as they fled the scene of a mass shooting at bar near Johannesburg BySean Seddon and Thuthuka ZondiReporting fromJohannesburgTwenty-seven people have been killed in two mass shootings hours apart in South Africa, the latest gun violence to hit the country.Seventeen people were killed when eight gunmen stormed a bar close to Johannesburg on Saturday evening, with 15 more injured.In a separate shooting in the early hours of Sunday morning, 10 people were killed outside an entertainment venue in a township near Cape Town and a further 11 were injured.South Africa has one of the highest murder rates in the world and has struggled to contain gang-related gun violence in recent years. Police have not given a definitive motive for either of the latest attacks.In Johannesburg, gunmen opened fire on customers outside the bar before continuing the attack inside and torching two vehicles before fleeing.Police said thirteen men and four women were killed.Gauteng Police Commissioner Lt Gen Tommy Mthombeni said people from Ethiopia and Lesotho were among those caught up in the shooting, but that the vast majority were South African.He also said police believed the motive behind the shooting was robbery, as personal belongings had been taken, but that they could not rule out turf wars between illegal mining groups.Image caption, In Johannesburg, gunmen torched two vehicles after opened fire on customers at a bar.Mining without permission in abandoned shafts or dump sites is a persistent problem in South Africa, causing numerous deaths from unsafe conditions. Authorities say the miners are typically undocumented migrants recruited by organised crime gangs.Earlier, deputy provincial police commissioner Maj Gen Fred Kekana said the attack may have been a retaliation for an incident last month, in which one person was killed and another wounded, according to news agency AFP.He said at the scene that police had clear pictures and in some cases names for the suspects. In Cape Town, five victims died at the scene and a further five people died in hospital.No arrests had been made in either case on Sunday morning.The two mass shootings came days after 11 people were killed in the city of Durban when gunmen stormed a house in the KwaMakhutha township. Image source, ReutersImage caption, Armed security forces were deployed to the scene of a mass shooting in Cape Town on Sunday morningIllegal weapons are prevalent in South Africa and gun violence has been a persistent problem for decades, with about 60 murders recorded every day in the country of 62 million.But a recent spate of mass shootings has raised fears that the problem is deepening, with violence often linked to turf wars between gangs engaged in drug trafficking and illegal mining.In February, President Cyril Ramaphosa called organised crime \"the most immediate threat to our democracy, our society and our economic development\".Earlier this year, the military was deployed alongside police in areas with the highest murder rates in an effort to reduce armed violence.On Saturday, a tenth woman's body was found in Ekurhuleni, to the east of Johannesburg, with local police saying they were unable to rule out a serial killer operating in the area with similarities between the deaths.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-27T14:09:22.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/ccd0/live/83714c70-ba4b-11f1-952f-c5888b744c34.jpg",
