    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-26-celebrity-traitors-2-cast-who-are-this-year-s-big-dogs-and-d",
      "title": "Celebrity Traitors 2 cast: Who are this year's 'big dogs' and dark horses?",
      "summary": "The reality TV game show returns on Thursday, with 21 stars vying to win £100,000 for their chosen charity.",
      "body": "Celebrity Traitors returns next week for a second round of deception and amateur detective work.Hosted by Claudia Winkleman, the Traitors castle will welcome 21 new celebrities on Thursday, as a group of famous \"faithfuls\" attempts to work out which among them are - and perhaps, always have been - \"traitors\", secretly murdering contestants each night.Last year's show provided Bafta's most memorable TV moment - an award voted for by the British public - when traitor Alan Carr broke down in tears at the end of the final having bluffed his way to a win by hiding in plain sight.Let's have a stab in the dark, then, at who might follow in Jonathan Ross's footsteps as this year's \"big dog\", and who, like singer Cat Burns, could fly under the radar? If Carr was the star of last year's show, then perhaps his fellow TV funnymen could be the ones to watch this time around.Naturally good readers of a room and skilled at misdirection, comedians can inject some comic relief into those tense roundtable moments.It worked for Carr, who was considered to be less of a threat and more out of his depth by the other sleuths.This time around, stand-up star Joe Lycett, 38, is one of the leading candidates for the role of class clown. Known for his quick wit and political stunts, Lycett told the BBC he asked Carr for advice on \"how to spot a rotten little traitor like you\".Last year's winner replied: \"Just anyone that's sweating and giggling.\"With his brightly coloured outfits, Lycett will bring some glamour to the castle, if and when he's not donning the famous dark cloak.\"There's a lot of variety and silly stuff that I might not get to wear at all if I end up doing a Paloma,\" he noted, with reference to Paloma Faith, who was brutally killed off early doors last year by her supposed friend, Carr.If laughing all the way to the winning line is to work again, then it's hard to look beyond James Acaster.Anyone who has seen his Netflix special Repertoire or heard his podcast Off Menu will know his deadpan, absurdist humour and whimsical flights of fancy could make it difficult to tell whether he is telling the truth or not.A \"huge fan\" of the show since the first non-celebrity edition in 2022, Acaster, 41, said he would prefer to be a faithful as he wasn't good at lying - though Carr did previously say something similar...Romesh Ranganathan's dry humour and wry presenting style saw him succeed Anne Robinson as the host of The Weakest Link revival in 2021. Could it help him to become a more believable traitor or faithful?The former maths teacher and now comedy podcaster, 48, blamed his youngest son, who was \"desperate for me to come on\", for his reluctant participation this year.One of his best friends on the comedy circuit, Parenting Hell podcaster Rob Beckett, is another of this year's funniest contestants.A decade's worth of friendship with the 8 Out of 10 Cats and Mock the Week panellist could count for nothing in the castle, Ranganathan said. He believes Beckett, 40, is \"just as easily going to be on my side as he is going to twist the knife in my back given the opportunity\".One of the nation's other most celebrated comics, Miranda Hart will no doubt be a big personality among the group. In her self-titled sitcom, she starred as an awkward, accident-prone shopkeeper. Her apparent clumsiness and warm, self-deprecating persona might make her seem more trustworthy (rightly or wrongly).After a spell of ill-health, Hart, 53, hopes \"the joy of mucking about on television again will hopefully outweigh some of the challenges\".Irish comedian Joanne McNally, 43, could also be the joker of the pack this year. Her popular podcast My Therapist Ghosted Me is full of gossip, oversharing and comic bickering - music to the ears of the Traitors producers, no doubt.Her stand-up is confessional and brutally honest. But honesty gets you nowhere in this game - or does it?The \"Big Dog Theory\" was a piece of gameplay logic proposed by ex-rugby union star Joe Marler during roundtable discussions last year.His reasoning was that one of the most famous contestants, AKA the big dogs, would have been chosen as a traitor to cause mass confusion (and, presumably, strong viewing figures).Marler wondered out loud whether the big dog traitor in question was Sir Stephen Fry (no) or Jonathan Ross (yes). For the new series, a glut of huge names and personalities from across the worlds of film, TV, music, modelling and broadcasting have also been selected.Michael Sheen, the award-winning Welsh actor and campaigner, is perhaps the biggest dog in the fight, so will come under real scrutiny.Sheen, 57, is acclaimed for his ability to convincingly portray a range of different personalities on screen, from Tony Blair to Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor via Brian Clough.But in real life, he's seen more as a loveable golden retriever than a Rottweiler, despite his success. Which version of him we shall see on the show remains to be seen.Fellow big screen charmer Richard E. Grant, 69, who starred in films such as Withnail and I, may also leave some of the less well-known celebrities star-struck at first, as might American-British supermodel/actress Jerry Hall.Hall, 70, shares four children with ex-partner Mick Jagger and has also been married to media mogul Rupert Murdoch.Love Island presenter Maya Jama, 32, and University Challenge host, journalist and YouTuber Amol Rajan are also powerhouses in the world of broadcasting.Speaking ahead of the show, 43-year-old Rajan, who is in it to win it, said : \"I don't want to go in there and at round table number one be Mr Forensic Investigator! \"However, I do interview politicians for a living, and I really have to pursue answers to questions.\"He's conscious of putting \"a target\" on his back too soon, so plans to form alliances.\"To begin with, I want to try and make friends and keep my head down,\" he added.EastEnders hardman turned hard-hitting documentarian Ross Kemp, 62, might prove popular once the tasks get physical.And we don't know yet whether to expect the soppy You're Beautiful balladeer James Blunt, 52, or the prolific (and hilarious) online troll of the same name.Either way, if the Big Dog Theory holds true, then one of the above will surely find themselves in the traitors' turret before long.Little Mix and solo singing star Leigh-Anne Pinnock, 34, may be a big dog in the world of pop music, but she comes across as a down-to-earth family woman these days.Don't be fooled, though, as she has a wealth of experience of managing relationships under intense public pressure from her girl group days.Meanwhile, another potential dark horse, Bella Ramsey, revealed they had been playing a version of The Traitors since they were 10 years old, even hosting events at home - so should be well-prepared.Now 22, the Last of Us star, who also featured in Game of Thrones, is the youngest of this year's contestants but should not be underestimated. The Traitors castle should be a cakewalk compared with the apocalypse or the Battle of Winterfell, right? The plan is to be \"instinctive, measured, and perceptive\", Ramsey said. \"I will be keeping my opinions to myself until I feel it's absolutely the right time to share them.\" King Kenny, a member of popular British YouTube collective Beta Squad, is known for pranks, vlogs and more recently (and briefly), boxing. The 29-year-old will be hoping the class of 2026 has more faith in online influencers than last year, when his Beta Squadmate Niko Omilana was the first player banished from the castle.Omilana's early exit also sparked a discussion among some critics about non-white contestants being voted off UK/US reality game shows at an early stage, and whether unconscious bias may play a role.Mathematician and broadcaster, Hannah Fry, who co-hosts The Rest is Science podcast, could fill David Olusoga's intellectual shoes this year.Historian Olusoga quietly made it to the final last year despite having repeatedly voted out faithfuls thinking they were traitors.Fry, 42, said she was \"here to fulfil the nerd quota\" and was taking the responsibility \"extremely seriously\".She added: \"I would say people tend to think I'm smarter than I actually am, bluntly, and I don't think that's going to serve me particularly well as I may be seen as a bit of a threat.\"If the comedians are aiming to be the next Carr, then who could be the new Cat Burns and fly under the radar all the way to the final?US actress Myha'la, 30, who plays Harper Stern in banking bonkbuster series Industry, may be top pick.Unlike her ruthless character on the show, she said she was more of a \"kill them with kindness kind of girl rather than a blatantly bullish bulldog\". She added: \"All that said, I want to be a Traitor. Obviously!\"She's deliberately planned her look - clothes, hair and jewellery - to separate herself from Stern and \"be seen as me and not that character\".Julie Hesmondhalgh, will be known to audiences Up North especially as Roy Cropper's wife Hayley in the soap Coronation Street, between 1998 and 2014. Hesmondhalgh, 56, who also appeared in Broadchurch and Mr Bates vs the Post Office, won't struggle to make friends with her warm personality. But they'd be wise not to forget she is, after all, a talented and experienced actress.Sharon Rooney, star of My Mad Fat Diary and the Barbie film, is another contestant likely to keep a low profile, which, as we've seen, can work well up to a point. But will it trigger suspicion of the Scottish Lawyer Barbie actress, 37, eventually?And last but not least, Sebastian Croft, star of the recent Netflix coming-of-age hit Heartstopper, rounds off our list. Younger viewers will know all about Croft, 24, already, but will the older contestants?Some names are bigger than others right now, but as three of last year's finalists - Burns, Olusoga and Nick Mohammed - discovered, that could change quickly over the coming episodes.The Celebrity Traitors returns to BBC One and iPlayer on Thursday, 1 October.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-26T23:01:47.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/10ab/live/967f1e10-b8c4-11f1-b1d1-571ed4d7ff2c.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cv5yjzz803edo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 8,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-26-faisal-islam-the-two-big-decisions-the-chancellor-must-make",
      "title": "Faisal Islam: The two big decisions the chancellor must make",
      "summary": "He must consider the longevity of Iran war economic pressures and how to sustain modest optimism, writes the BBC's Faisal Islam.",
      "body": "\"It is tough,\" John Healey admitted to me earlier this month when I asked him if there had been too much doom-mongering?\"Conflicts, uncertainty, driving up inflation, driving up interest rates. But we've got great strengths. We've got good reasons to be confident about the future of Britain,\" he insisted.The new scheme to try and help young people get on the property ladder, announced on Saturday, was aimed at boosting confidence in the economy.But the chancellor is now facing two big decisions before his inaugural Budget on 28 October.Firstly, about the longevity of the economic pressures caused by the Iran War, and then about how to sustain a modest but notable uptick in economic spirits, through yet more global turbulence.In his first weeks as chancellor the oil price fell as low as $75 a barrel while the yield - the effective interest rate - on 10-year government bonds was 4.9%.Just over two months later oil has largely traded above $100 and the 10-year yield is at around 5.4%. It is the ultimate double whammy for a chancellor facing his first Budget.Yet there is something unusual about this energy shock. It could quickly reverse, as illustrated earlier in the summer when assumptions about a de-escalation in the US Iran conflict led to sharp falls in energy prices and yields.In New York last week both President Trump and his Iranian counterpart President Pezeshkian suggested November's US midterm elections were connected to when the war might end.The Iranians would wait until after the elections to seek peace because the war's impact on the cost of living - particularly soaring diesel costs - could damage him electorally, the US president told the UN General Assembly.The Iranian president said his nation \"didn't want it to get to the midterms\".Both sides in this war are feeling the economic squeeze of conflict. The vote on 3 November is six days after the Budget. No-one could rely on there being a settlement by then, but it is plausible.So the Budget tax, spend and borrowing forecasts could be based on a prolonged conflict, which is actually on the cusp of ending, or even already ended. The chancellor has to therefore decide whether to plan for the worst, and make some painful permanent tax and spend decisions, or buy some time.One option is to let borrowing take some of the strain, by tolerating a reduction in the £24bn of headroom - room for manoeuvre against the government's self-imposed borrowing rules - left by his predecessor, Rachel Reeves.This year's headroom will be judged in three years rather than four, so there is some rationale as to why a smaller number over a closer target might be appropriate.And while higher inflation means higher interest costs, it also means higher cash tax receipts, at a time when thresholds continue to be frozen.There is a second consideration. The hopeful optimism \"vibes\" strategy of the Burnham administration appears to have had some impact.The longest-running UK consumer confidence survey has hit a two-year high. Among younger people it has not been this high since before Brexit.Some data companies call it a \"Burnham bounce\", although the weather and World Cup were also factors.There is evidence, more mixed, that business optimism has also perked-up in recent months, possibly because of earlier falls in energy prices, though this has been clouded by anticipation of possible tax rises. The Institute of Directors business group said this was despite, rather than because of, the new government.While no-one should get too carried away with these turnarounds, they do stand in contrast to the admitted mistake of the Starmer government in talking up consumer pain two years ago. The question for the chancellor is whether better consumer and business spirits can be reconciled with the need for a \"challenging\" Budget?Indeed, it's important to determine how much of the summer global bond market shock is solely down to the impact of the Iran war and how much is more structural.Governments are facing new and intense competition in bond markets from the world's biggest AI businesses. In addition, the UK has had recent, prominent political and economic uncertainty.Right now the bond markets are like a pack of wolves stalking red deer, probing for weaknesses.\"At times like this you don't want to be at the back of the herd,\" said one very senior former Treasury adviser. In response, the Treasury has pointed to the UK having the highest growth and the fastest falling borrowing of the major G7 countries so far this year and our overall energy prices starting to decouple from high and volatile gas prices.However, the IMF chief Kristalina Georgieva this week dismissed the idea that advanced nations could pause their fiscal homework, while the Gulf situation calmed.\"Bring debt levels down, make fiscal consolidation as a priority… it is impossible to stress strongly enough how critical it is to get the courage to take the steps that are necessary,\" Georgieva told me, acknowledging the UK's \"fairly consistent, credible\" plans.Then there is the curious conundrum of British productivity. Last year, the government's official forecaster the Office for Budget Responsibility (OBR) downgraded the UK's productivity peformance, leading to a notable hit to the public finances.Now the UK's productivity record has been upgraded materially by the Office for National Statistics, although this is because of fewer hours worked. There could therefore be an argument to reverse some of that downgrade.Separately, the new chair of the OBR Jonathan Haskel is known for his argument that official statistics understate investment in intangibles like software and data. His latest research suggests AI-related investment is already visible in US productivity figures, and he has long been optimistic about tech's impact on the future.While it seems unlikely that such factors will make it into the OBR's calculations next month, there is a real debate here. Officially, however, part of the job at the Budget is to identify cuts to fund the Defence Investment Plan, inherited from Sir Keir. That's before funding the additional move to 3% of GDP and a new social care system. Even a promise of less welfare spending looks like being built on an upfront investment in jobs for young people.One change from last year is that so far, the government has parked the \"pitch-rolling\" with a silence that would rival the Trappist monks.Instead, policy announcements are strategically timed such as the \"Your First Home\" scheme just as the Labour party conference got underway. We can expect to hear plenty more as the Budget nears.",
