      "id": "theverge-2026-09-24-qualcomm-s-new-elite-sound-chip-might-finally-deliver-the-wi",
      "title": "Qualcomm’s new ‘Elite’ sound chip might finally deliver the Wi-Fi earbud dream",
      "summary": "What if your wireless earbuds - or audio glasses - could stream high-quality lossless audio that doesn't cut out when you leave your phone on the bedside charger or buried in the couch? Qualcomm's new Snapdragon Sound Elite Gen 2 is its first to integrate \"micro-power Wi-Fi 6E\" right into the chip, so they can connect directly to your home Wi-Fi network, and from there to the cloud.\nIf that sounds",
      "body": "What if your wireless earbuds — or audio glasses — could stream high-quality lossless audio that doesn’t cut out when you leave your phone on the bedside charger or buried in the couch? Qualcomm’s new Snapdragon Sound Elite Gen 2 is its first to integrate “micro-power Wi-Fi 6E” right into the chip, so they can connect directly to your home Wi-Fi network, and from there to the cloud.\n[Image: Qualcomm’s old whitepaper shows that it originally required two chips for Wi-Fi earbuds. Now, Wi-Fi is integrated into the main chip. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/image-26.png?quality=90&strip=all]\nIf that sounds like a familiar promise, it’s probably because Qualcomm promised something similar back in 2023 with the “S7 Pro sound platform” and its Expanded Personal Area Network (XPAN) tech, saying that Wi-Fi earbuds can do what Bluetooth alone cannot. Namely, 24-bit 96kHz lossless audio that can’t fit into Bluetooth’s meager bandwidth.\nBut that old 7-series platform required a separate Wi-Fi chip to give you Wi-Fi earbuds, and it seems that wasn’t a winning formula for getting makers of incredibly tiny devices to adopt the tech! In fact, we’ve only ever heard of one: Xiaomi’s 2025 Buds 5 Pro, which didn’t get a global release.\nNow that Wi-Fi no longer requires an extra chip, perhaps the idea can take off. Qualcomm says the Elite is also 30 percent smaller, consumes up to 40 percent less power, and has twice the AI processing capabilities of the previous gen chip, all of which should be music to device makers’ ears.\nSo far, Qualcomm says it’s working with HP on one specific unnamed product, is “working closely with Bose,” and is “pioneering a new category of intelligent hearables” (read: earbuds with built-in cameras) with Cleer.\n[Image: The camera earbuds. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/vlcsnap-2026-09-24-16h35m50s149.png?quality=90&strip=all]",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Gadgets",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Sean Hollister",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-24T23:42:39.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/vlcsnap-2026-09-24-16h29m15s548.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,3.4613147178592,100,93.077370564282",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/1000443/qualcomms-new-elite-sound-chip-might-finally-deliver-the-wi-fi-earbud-dream",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
