      "id": "bbc-2026-09-24-the-compromises-and-power-struggles-that-shaped-fury-joshua",
      "title": "The compromises and power struggles that shaped Fury-Joshua",
      "summary": "BBC Sport takes a look at the compromises, power struggles and competing interests behind Tyson Fury v Anthony Joshua.",
      "body": "After a decade of stalled negotiations, public squabbling and false starts, Tyson Fury and Anthony Joshua will finally meet in the ring on 11 December at Cardiff's Principality Stadium.BBC Sport understands the WBA 'Super' heavyweight title is expected to be on the line, although confirmation from the governing body is still awaited.It would give both former world champions the chance to regain a version of the heavyweight crown, although Fury-Joshua has always been about more than belts.The fight arrives later than anybody wanted, with both in the twilight of their careers, but for years British boxing's biggest heavyweight rivalry seemed destined to remain a story of what might have been.Yet Thursday's announcement of an 80,000-capacity showdown, broadcast globally by Netflix, did more than finally provide a date and venue.It also offered a glimpse of the competing interests, egos and power struggles involved in getting one of boxing's most elusive fights over the line.Speaking to BBC Sport after the fight was announced, Fury's promoter Frank Warren repeatedly returned to one word: compromise.\"Everybody's got to make a compromise,\" the Queensberry boss said. \"Tyson really compromised. He didn't dig his heels in.\"He said, 'Fine, wherever they want it, I'll do it. If they want me to go to New York, I'll go. They want it in the UK, I just want to get the fight on.\"The fighters had already agreed to the contest but the venue was a sticking point.Warren says Netflix wanted New York, while Joshua, 36, was determined to fight in Britain. Predictably, their camps disagree over who finally got it done.Joshua's promoter Eddie Hearn described the push to keep the fight in Britain as a \"two-man crusade\".\"AJ has turned down money to keep it in the UK,\" Hearn told the Matchroom YouTube channel. \"I wouldn't be able to look people in the eye if I was part of the movement to take this to the US.\"Hearn says Joshua had specifically requested protection for a UK fight in his contract and refused to relent.\"We were under so much pressure to fold,\" he added. \"It was only really the support of the British public that made Joshua stand 10 toes down and say no.\"Warren sees it differently.He says Fury, 38, could have insisted on his own contractual position but instead accepted Britain to ensure the fight did not collapse.Perhaps the biggest sideshow has been the struggle for influence around the promotion itself.Saudi boxing powerbroker Turki Alalshikh announced the fight, having previously insisted any confirmation would come from him.\"For 15 years no-one has been able to make this fight,\" he wrote. \"Back in April, I said that any announcement would come from me. I can now confirm that Fury v Joshua will be coming to the UK, as I promised British boxing fans. The time for talking is over.\"Dana White's involvement is particularly intriguing.The UFC chief is partnered with Alalshikh through Zuffa Boxing and featured prominently in the announcement, with Netflix describing the event as a collaboration involving White and Alalshikh's The Ring. Hearn and Warren were absent.Hearn had previously said White would not be involved in promoting the fight, while Warren says both fighters' contracts stipulated the same.Yet Warren says White ultimately helped secure the Netflix deal but believes fans care little about the promotional politics.\"I don't know whether he will be front and centre or not,\" Warren said.Wednesday's news conference in London may tell us more - not least how prominent White is and how he and Hearn share the stage after months of back-and-forth.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-24T18:41:35.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/60d8/live/f284d560-b842-11f1-bc1f-3f186ca4140c.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/boxing/articles/c65y74pjl0w4o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-24-watch-un-delegates-walk-out-of-netanyahu-speech",
      "title": "Watch: UN delegates walk out of Netanyahu speech",
      "summary": "The Israeli delegation could be be seen cheering in support during Netanyahu's speech.",
      "body": "Dozens of United Nations delegates walked out of the hall Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu was due to deliver a speech in.Addressing the UN's General Assembly in New York, Netanyahu described those walking out as \"moral cowards\". Those who remained and felt similarly should leave, he said.The Israeli delegation could be seen cheering Netanyahu in support.Israel has faced condemnation for its actions in Gaza and Lebanon. A UN commission of inquiry has previously accused Israel of committing genocide against Palestinians in Gaza - Israel has strongly rejected the accusations.The war was triggered by the Hamas-led attack on southern Israel on 7 October 2023, in which about 1,200 people were killed and 251 others were taken hostage.Israel responded by launching a military campaign in Gaza, during which more than 73,920 people have been killed, according to the territory's health ministry.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-24T18:29:29.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/cbc7/live/4d40df20-b844-11f1-b1d1-571ed4d7ff2c.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cqwyzmpm3pgdo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-24-stable-yc-w20-is-hiring-product-engineers",
      "title": "Stable (YC W20) Is Hiring Product Engineers",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.usestable.com/careers/product-engineer\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49834893\nPoints: 0\n# Comments: 0",
      "body": "📬 About StableOur mission is to make it simple to headquarter any business on the internet. Today, we provide companies with a business address and a dashboard to manage their physical mail online. Over 15,000+ companies like Brex, Doordash, and Gusto use Stable to automate their mailroom and act as their permanent business address with the IRS, state, and vendors.The rules that regulate US entities were written in the 1800s. Stable abstracts these antiquated requirements with tools that empower modern companies to move forward faster.These rules don't make sense for the way we work today — work takes place in the cloud and businesses are no longer tied to physical proximity or geography.We’re on a mission to fix the broken system of entity management. Starting with business addresses and mail, we’re abstracting the complex, archaic systems that make company-building painful and turning them into delightful experiences — so that modern businesses have the tools they need to move forward faster.We're backed by leading Silicon Valley investors like Y Combinator, Craft Ventures, Shakti, Hustle Fund, and founders from companies like Lattice, Apartment List, and FlexJobs.Our business is at an inflection point. We’re growing quickly with a product people love and we’ve proven we can service companies of all stages and industries — from early-stage startups to publicly traded companies in industries like technology, logistics, and property management.This is an opportunity to join an early-stage startup as one of the first employees and do work that directly impacts the future of how companies are built.👩‍💻👨‍💻 RoleWe’re looking for a product engineer with 3+ years of experience to join our small team and help us build the software backbone of modern business infrastructure.Supporting 15,000+ businesses with a lean team means we focus on impact. Engineers talk to users, identify real-world bottlenecks, and ship code that unlocks speed and scale. There’s no one handing you tickets—you’ll play a role in both defining what matters and building it.You’ll work across the full stack—frontend, backend, and occasionally hardware. The problems are tangible and the feedback is fast: you might train the AI model that detects a check inside a document, then write the integration that extracts the data to deposit it, then watch it run in a real warehouse the same week. You’ll have a direct hand in shaping the core systems that power our product and logistics. Some examples of what you might work on:Stable Dashboard - Our customers’ central hub for business operations. They login to automate their mail, manage registered agents, reconcile payments, and set up workflows that help their team move faster.Mail Operations - Hardware integrations and internal software that powers how we intake, route, and fulfill customer requests.Automation & AI - LLMs and ML models that route mail, classify documents, extract data, and trigger workflows.API & Webhooks - Design and maintain a reliable, well-documented API and webhook system that external teams use to integrate with Stable. Supports high-volume use cases in fintech, healthcare, logistics, and more.This role is for someone who enjoys building practical products that solve real problems. You're comfortable with ambiguity, excited to talk to customers, and ship code with clear, measurable impact.‍😀 What you valueBe human: Lead with empathy, act with authenticity, and enjoy what you do.Stay curious: Invent novel solutions by asking why, listening, and tinkering.Act quickly with purpose: Focus on what matters, iteratively improve, and move urgently towards the goal.Insist on exceptional outcomes: Strive for excellence and take ownership over the outcomes you deliver.Exceed customer expectations: Create delightful experiences with each interaction.‍✅ What you'll doObsess over the customer to build pragmatic solutions to their problemsLead end-to-end implementation of features, from discovery through implementationDesign software that solves real-world logistics challenges—often involving both automation and human-in-the-loop workflowsArchitect and implement core infrastructure that helps us scale both customer and mail volumeBalance technical complexity with input from customers, operations, and the businessTechnologies we use: React, Typescript, Node, GraphQL, MySQL, and AWS (if you think a new technology can solve an engineering problem, we’re all ears)‍✨ Requirements3+ years of experience as a product engineer, full stack engineer, or similarEvidence of customer empathy and business context—you understand who you build for, why, and what impact it droveSelf-directed and comfortable leading projects end-to-end with loosely defined scopeStrong communication skills—you can explain technical concepts to non-engineersComfortable with ambiguity and a fast-paced, high-growth environmentTravel at least quarterly for company offsitesNice to have: Experience in startup environments or early-stage product workNice to have: Familiarity with warehouse or fulfillment center technology‍🎁 What we offerCompetitive salary and generous equity 🚀Unlimited paid vacation 🏖Medical, dental, and vision insurance 🏥Home office set-up 🖥Work from anywhere within US time zones (GMT-5 to GMT-10) 💻Opportunities to shape the future of Stable and grow into leadership roles 💌",
      "source": "Hacker News",
