      "id": "bbc-2026-09-24-man-jailed-for-two-years-over-throwing-bottle-at-portsmouth-",
      "title": "Man jailed for two years over throwing bottle at Portsmouth migrant protest",
      "summary": "Del Somerville, 42, pleaded guilty to violent disorder following an overnight protest at Eastney.",
      "body": "Judge Daniel Sawyer told Somerville that his sentence would act as a deterrent to others following subsequent protests in the area.Describing him as a \"thug\", he told the defendant: \"Police officers dedicate their lives to the protection of the public regardless of their own views, the very least they can expect is that those who engage in protests treat them with respect and the courts punish those who assault them.\"The court heard Sommerville, from Portsmouth, was among a large group of demonstrators protesting at the arrival of the migrants.They were brought ashore by the RNLI after being picked up in the Channel. A number of volunteer lifeboat crew members were subject to online abuse following the incident.Prosecutor Edward Warren said Somerville was part of an \"initially peaceful and lawful\" gathering of a number of people on Ferry Road.At shortly before 23:30 BST, he was with a large group of men as they were held up by the police line.The court was told he was identified in police body-worn video as throwing a small bottle and swearing at police.When later interviewed by police, he said he had found the bottle on the ground and was going to throw it into a nearby bush, but was angry and so threw it up into the air.Defence barrister Helen Easterbrook said Somerville had been \"caught up\" in the disturbances.She said he had been attempting to be a \"calming influence\" on the crowd but had thrown the bottle \"in frustration\".Four other men are awaiting sentence having also been charged with violent disorder following the protest in Eastney.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-24T15:57:55.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/9660/live/3a490bd0-b831-11f1-b1d1-571ed4d7ff2c.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c8vgy7ww21rqo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-24-on-the-run-killer-gary-anderson-appears-in-court",
      "title": "On-the-run killer Gary Anderson appears in court",
      "summary": "Gary Anderson had been serving life in prison for the murder of Karol Kelly in Londonderry in 2018.",
      "body": "Image source, NorthWestNewsPixImage caption, Gary Anderson, pictured outside a special sitting of Letterkenny District Court on Thursday, following his arrest in County DonegalByMike McBride BBC News NIAn on-the-run killer who absconded after being permitted to attend his father's funeral in Northern Ireland 10 days ago has appeared in court in the Republic of Ireland, where he was arrested on Wednesday.Gary Anderson, 29, appeared at a special sitting of Letterkenny District Court on Thursday following his arrest at a campsite near Buncrana, by An Garda Síochána (Irish police).He had been serving life in prison for the murder of Karol Kelly in Londonderry in 2018.The court was told Anderson is charged with being present at Fahan Marina caravan and campsite on 23 September, having escaped from Maghaberry Prison, contrary to Section 3(1) of the Criminal Law (Jurisdiction) Act 1976.Image source, Family handoutImage caption, Karol Kelly was stabbed to death in Londonderry in 2018Garda Det Jason Conroy gave details of the arrest, charge and caution during the hearing on Thursday.The detective said Anderson was arrested at about 13:44 local time on Wednesday at Fahan Marina campsite and was told why he was being arrested but made no reply.At 20:47, he was charged, handed a copy of the charge sheet and again made no reply, the court was told.Garda Sgt Jim Collins told the court that there had been no application for bail from Anderson's legal representatives at that time.Image caption, The court was told Gary Anderson was arrested at Fahan Marina caravan and campsite on 23 SeptemberCollins said he was seeking directions from the Director of Public Prosecutions (DPP) in the case and was seeking to have the accused remanded in custody.He applied to Judge Emile Daly for an adjournment in the case to allow for these directions.Judge Daly adjourned the case for Anderson to appear by videolink at Letterkenny District Court later this month.His solicitor said that while he understood Judge Daly could not direct that Anderson be held in a prison other than Castlerea Prison in County Roscommon, he asked the judge to recommend another prison because of what he termed \"other parties involved\".On Thursday Karol Kelly's sister Francesca confirmed that she met the Justice Minister Naomi Long to discuss the matter.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-24T15:51:28.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/5bd3/live/65b408b0-b809-11f1-93f5-ff96839fb91e.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ck5yl06j3ezeo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-24-reddit-mod-ordered-to-pay-nintendo-4-5-million-in-switch-pir",
      "title": "Reddit mod ordered to pay Nintendo $4.5 million in Switch piracy lawsuit",
      "summary": "Nintendo won its $4.5 million lawsuit against James Williams, a Reddit moderator accused of distributing pirated Switch games, as reported earlier by Aftermath. In a default judgment issued on Wednesday, a Washington federal judge also ordered Williams to shut down the online stores allegedly housing libraries of pirated Switch games.\nNintendo sued Williams, also known as \"archbox\" online, in 2024",
      "body": "Nintendo won its $4.5 million lawsuit against James Williams, a Reddit moderator accused of distributing pirated Switch games, as reported earlier by Aftermath. In a default judgment issued on Wednesday, a Washington federal judge also ordered Williams to shut down the online stores allegedly housing libraries of pirated Switch games.\nNintendo sued Williams, also known as “archbox” online, in 2024. In its complaint, the gaming giant claimed Williams became a “leading moderator” of the r/SwitchPirates community, where he allegedly solicited “donations” of Nintendo eShop gift cards and copies of Switch games “to be copied and distributed” on online pirate stores. Last October, Nintendo asked the court for a $4.5 million default judgment after Williams didn’t respond to the lawsuit.\n“NOA [Nintendo of America] has demonstrated that Williams promoted online shops offering pirated Nintendo game files and circumvention software, encouraged others to contribute pirated Nintendo games to his pirate shops, and assisted others in distributing pirated Nintendo Switch games, even though he knew such activity violated copyright laws,” US District Judge Lauren King writes in the order.\nThis marks yet another legal victory for Nintendo, which has filed a flurry of copyright lawsuits over the past several years. The company most recently won a court case in the Netherlands, which deemed it illegal to sell Mig flash cartridges that allow people to play pirated copies of games.\nThe $4.5 million award reflects $150,000 per violation across 30 copyrighted games, including Animal Crossing: New Horizons, Luigi’s Mansion 3, and Pikmin 4. Williams must also “immediately disable access” to his alleged pirate shops, as well as shut down any social media accounts used to distribute copyrighted Switch games.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Copyright",
        "Gaming",
        "Law",
        "News",
        "Nintendo",
        "Policy"
      ],
      "author": "Emma Roth",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-24T15:50:35.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/chorus/uploads/chorus_asset/file/13695478/akrales_190116_3173_0011.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/games/1000088/nintendo-switch-piracy-reddit-mod-lawsuit",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-24-rogue-openai-agent-infiltrated-australian-government-website",
      "title": "Rogue OpenAI agent 'infiltrated' Australian government website in world first",
      "summary": "Australia criticised OpenAI for taking \"too long\" to tell them about the breach which happened in June.",
      "body": "A rogue OpenAI agent hacked an Australian government website in June and accessed private data in what experts say is the first known case of its kind in the world.The agent \"infiltrated\" a statistics portal containing \"non-sensitive\" data from Australia's universal healthcare scheme Medicare, Prime Minister Anthony Albanese said in New York on Wednesday, local time.He had a \"very frank discussion\" with OpenAI boss Sam Altman for taking \"too long\" to disclose the breach and said there would be \"legal consequences\".OpenAI said it only learnt of the breach in August while reviewing \"misaligned model activity\" and emailed a general inbox of an Australian government agency on 10 September.Five days later, that government agency, Services Australia, escalated the email to Australia's cybersecurity centre before a government minister was notified and the prime minister alerted.Albanese said he spoke to Altman and raised \"Australia's extreme concern about this incident\" as well as his \"disappointment\" that the company had taken months to reveal the breach and \"the nature of the way\" it did so.The Australian leader said Altman had acknowledged there were \"issues with protocols\" at OpenAI.A \"forensic investigation\" led by the country's cybersecurity agency would aim to find out if other government systems were affected, Albanese said.The probe would also assess if the matter needed to be dealt with by police, he said, noting there \"will obviously be legal consequences\".Detailing the breach, Albanese said it had involved \"public and non-public files\" on the Medicare Statistics Reporting Service portal, home to \"non-sensitive\" data and statistics.Three other government systems \"may\" also have been affected: the Australian Institute of Health and Welfare and two state-based agencies - the New South Wales Bureau of Crime Statistics and Research and the Victorian Department of Health.\"No personal information is believed to have been accessed at this stage, but investigations are ongoing,\" Albanese said.\"Nonetheless this situation is obviously unacceptable,\" he said.OpenAI, in a statement, said it had \"identified activity involving several Australian government websites and services as our models attempted to look up answers, and available statistics for questions about Australia during an internal evaluation\". \"In the course of that, our models took actions we did not intend,\" the statement said.It has also emerged that OpenAI's systems tried, and failed, to hack a digital library at the University of New Mexico in May, according to Transluce, a not-for-profit AI research lab.It said the systems also attempted to hack Data USA, a repository of public government data that same month. This also failed.Albanese declined to answer whether he raised the matter with US President Donald Trump during their face-to-face meeting on Tuesday night in New York, where world leaders have gathered for the UN General Assembly.Australia was one of 22 countries that earlier this week signed a joint statement calling for global oversight and guardrails for the development of AI. Cybersecurity experts told the BBC the incident is a wake up call for regulators, given that AI agents are becoming more widely available for individual and commercial use.Dr Hammond Pearce, senior lecturer at the University of NSW Institute for Cyber Security, told the BBC that though this is the first known incident where AI agents have chosen to breach a government body of their own volition, there'll be more to come.\"I expect that these kinds of attacks will keep occurring,\" he said, adding that they would likely \"grow in severity and in frequency\".\"I do hope that this incident does start ringing alarm bells in governments around the world.\"Earlier this year, OpenAI revealed a group of AI agents it had been testing had escaped from their controls and secretly worked together to hack another tech firm named Hugging Face.And a string of other rogue AI incidents have also been made public this year, including a case where a digital assistant - without instruction - booted someone off a pilates class waiting list in a bid to get an Australian man in.Several AI firm leaders themselves - including Altman, Anthropic's Dario Amodei, and Elon Musk - have said the speed at which AI is developing is dangerous to humanity and needs to be reined in.But the US and China, who are vying for AI supremacy, are roadblocks. Both are hostile to greater regulation, wanting the economic and technological spoils of AI, and have downplayed safety concerns.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "tech",
