      "id": "techcrunch-2026-09-24-australia-to-investigate-if-openai-hack-of-government-health",
      "title": "Australia to investigate if OpenAI hack of government health website broke the law",
      "summary": "The incident is the first known breach to affect a government agency, and Australia's prime minister has vowed to hold OpenAI accountable.",
      "body": "An OpenAI model hacked into an Australian government website, the country’s prime minister Anthony Albanese said Wednesday, in the first publicly reported case of an AI model hacking into a government’s systems.\nAlbanese said that there would “obviously be legal consequences” following the breach, and that OpenAI faces a government investigation into how its unreleased models gained access to reams of bulk health data information.\nThis latest incident disclosure comes as governments and tech companies grapple with how to rein in increasingly autonomous AI after a recent spate of AI agents breaking out of their sandboxes, colluding on the internet, and posing cybersecurity issues.\nThe breach also poses questions about how both OpenAI and the Australian government failed to detect the attack until several months later.\nDuring a Wednesday news briefing at the U.N. General Assembly, Albanese said that the breach began on June 18, but that OpenAI did not notify the government until September 10.\nOpenAI only became aware of the incident in August when it turned up during a broader, companywide review of agents behaving in unintended ways, according to an OpenAI spokesperson who reached TechCrunch via email.\nThe unspecified OpenAI agent obtained both public and nonpublic files from Services Australia, which administers Australia’s universal healthcare scheme. While the prime minister said there is no evidence that any citizens’ personal information was leaked, OpenAI said that the information the agent reached included aggregate health statistics and internal file names.\nThe agent was running during an internal OpenAI evaluation, seeking answers about Australia and publicly available medicine information. At the Medicare portal, the agent encountered repeated blocks but found ways around them.\nAlbanese told reporters that the model “didn’t accept no for an answer,” and added that the model had actively written data to the government’s database, rather than just accessing it, indicating the possibility that the department’s data was modified or muddied.\nThe prime minister said OpenAI disclosed the breach by sending a notification to the public mailbox of Services Australia, which then notified Australia’s Cyber Security Centre five days later. It’s unclear why there was a delay, but Albanese said he raised the breach directly with OpenAI chief executive Sam Altman by stressing Australia’s “extreme concern” about this incident and “disappointment” that OpenAI sat on the information for nearly three months.\n“This situation is obviously unacceptable,” said Albanese, making clear that he held the company accountable for both the hack and how slowly it came to light.\nAlbanese said that the government’s investigation will consider law enforcement and legislative responses to prevent incidents like this one happening again.\nAustralian media outlet ABC News reports that the latest identified attack may have relied on an earlier breach of a German wiki site, which was used as a staging ground for attacking the Australian government’s website. The AI model agents reportedly used the German wiki to leave notes to be used in later hacks, including a note to obtain data from the Australian Institute of Health and Welfare, a federal agency that publishes national health data. The agency is one of three additional systems that Albanese said may have been breached.\nTransluce, a nonprofit AI research lab, separately found public records showing AI agents targeting the Australian Institute of Health and Welfare on June 20 and 21.\nOpenAI did not respond to TechCrunch’s specific inquiry on whether the incidents were connected but acknowledged their “activity involving several Australian government websites and services.”\nThe incident comes after a string of security incidents caused by rogue agents, often acting within the infrastructure of AI labs. In July, swarms of OpenAI agents breached Hugging Face. Since then, more incidents of AI agent hacks from Anthropic, Meta, and Google have been revealed.\nOpenAI now says it is conducting an “extensive review of misaligned model activity during training and evaluation” and is notifying third parties of potential breaches.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nAditya Mehta is a reporter at TechCrunch covering AI. He’s supported by the Tarbell Center for AI Journalism and attended UC Berkeley. You can contact from Aditya by emailing aditya.mehta@techcrunch.com or via encrypted message at adymehta.74 on Signal.\nHe can be reached via encrypted message at zackwhittaker.1337 on Signal. You can also contact him by email, or to verify outreach, at zack.whittaker@techcrunch.com.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Aditya Mehta, Zack Whittaker",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-24T12:54:19.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/albanese-2296078640.jpg?resize=1200,774",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/24/australia-to-investigate-if-openai-hack-of-government-health-website-broke-the-law/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-24-crashed-raf-jet-steered-to-avoid-built-up-areas-before-pilot",
      "title": "Crashed RAF jet steered to 'avoid built-up areas' before pilots ejected, commander says",
      "summary": "Gp Capt Gez Currie said the pilots are in hospital, and their families are with them.",
      "body": "Crashed RAF jet steered to 'avoid built-up areas' before pilots ejectedFigure caption, Moment pilots eject as RAF jet crashes in AngleseyByRob ThomasReporting fromon AngleseyMiriam BarkerBBC Wales and Rowenna HoskinAn RAF jet was steered to \"avoid built-up areas\" before two pilots ejected out of the aircraft, an air force commander has said.Gp Capt Gez Currie, Station Commander, said: \"The aircraft would have been aimed in a direction that the pilot believed was open field, in order to avoid built-up areas and anybody on the ground.\"The Hawk T2 aircraft had been flying out of the RAF Valley station on Anglesey on Wednesday when it plummeted to the ground in nearby Pencarnisiog, narrowly avoiding homes.The trainee and an experienced professional, who parachuted out of their plane moments before it crashed, are expected to make a full recovery after they were treated for minor injuries.Hawk operations have been paused by the RAF as a \"precautionary measure\" while investigations into the crash are ongoing - the Red Arrows are included in the pause as the aerobatics display team use the T1.The Ministry of Defence (MoD) confirmed that the squadron of Hawk jets at RAF Leeming, in Yorkshire, has also \"paused\" flying.Figure caption, Gp Cpt Gez Currie, Station Commander of RAF Valley, said the pilots are with their families Currie said the pilots were in hospital being cared for and were with their families. He would not give personal details about them and said the investigation was in its early stages, adding \"it's far too early to even suggest what has happened in this occasion\".He said: \"It was a trainee, with a highly experienced instructor on a routine sortie, across the Anglesey area.\"According to the MoD, a sortie is \"one mission, one complete trip\". He added that in the two-and-a-half years as station commander, and in his 30-year career as an air traffic controller, \"this is the first time I've experienced this\".Eyewitness Ollie Zalot said he was initially concerned that people on the ground could have been hurt as the crash happened near buildings, while Anglesey councillor Neville Evans said he thought it had been a \"very lucky escape considering how close\" the crash was to a village and caravan park.Image caption, The RAF plane crash happened near PencarnisiogA video of the crash shared on social media on Wednesday appeared to show the two pilots ejecting from the aircraft before it nosedives and crashes in flames.Separate footage also showed a plume of smoke and flames rising from the rear of a jet as it rolled down the runway before the crash. As the jet climbed, the flames seemed to disappear. Mikey Kay, a BBC security analyst who also served in the RAF for 20 years, told the BBC that while it was too early to say, it was possible that the cause of the crash was not an engine failure.In a statement on social media, the RAF said: \"Both pilots are doing well, they are in extremely good spirits and expected to make a full recovery.\"A formal investigation into the circumstances surrounding the incident is now under way and, as a precautionary measure, the RAF has temporarily paused Hawk T1 and Hawk T2 flying operations.\"Image gallerySkip image galleryImage source, Danni JonesImage caption, The two pilots were able to eject themselves before the plane crashedSlide 1 of 5, Two figures floating in the sky with parachutes, The two pilots were able to eject themselves before the plane crashedThe Hawk T1, and newer T2 versions are transonic, two-seat training aircraft used to train fast jet pilots.The Red Arrows, based at RAF Waddington in Lincolnshire, are included in the pause, however they were not due to fly again until Le Touquet Airshow in France this weekend in any event. It is not known when the restriction will be lifted.More top stories",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-24T12:29:39.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/fd40/live/0ea1fb80-b805-11f1-a430-4d16ee157c41.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/crx2zlqqpn2do?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
