      "id": "bbc-2026-09-22-lindsay-clancy-s-lawyer-asks-judge-to-investigate-juror-and-",
      "title": "Lindsay Clancy's lawyer asks judge to investigate juror and check his phone",
      "summary": "The attorney wants the court to look at the juror's mobile phone use and answers on questionnaires.",
      "body": "Image source, ReutersImage caption, Lindsay Clancy attorney Kevin Reddington made several attempts to convince the judge not to declare a mistrialThe defence lawyer for a Massachusetts mother accused of murdering her three children has asked a judge to investigate a juror in the case.The proceedings against Lindsay Clancy ended in a mistrial and acrimony this month after the jury failed to reach a unanimous verdict.Her attorney, Kevin Reddington, wants the court to probe mobile phone use by \"Juror Doe\" during deliberations as well as a questionnaire he filled out. A lawyer for Michael Desronvil, the lone juror who wanted to find Clancy guilty, said the latest defence motion was a \"Looney Tunes\" attack on his client for doing his civic duty. Desronvil has said there was \"enough proof that she [Clancy] knew exactly what she was doing\". Figure caption, Watch: Patrick Clancy says he forgives his ex-wife, LindsayWarning: This article contains distressing detailsThe juror forewoman has claimed in a media interview that Desronvil had expressed reasonable doubt about Clancy's guilt, which would require him to find her not guilty. He denies this.Clancy has not denied killing her three children, Cora, five, Dawson, three, and Callan, eight months, at the family's Massachusetts home. Her attorneys argued she was not guilty by reason of insanity, that she was struggling with postpartum psychosis at the time. Prosecutors said Clancy made a calculated decision to send her husband out of the house on an errand so she could strangle her children. In his motion filed on Friday, Reddington asked the court to look at calls and texts that \"Juror Doe\" made between 08:00 and 16:30 local time during all the days the jury deliberated. Image source, The Heard FoundationImage caption, Cora, five, Callan, eight months, and Dawson, three He also asked the court to examine the truthfulness of the answers the juror gave during the jury selection process, as well as his responses to prospective juror questionnaires. In a separate motion, Reddington also asked for the case against Clancy to be dismissed. Prosecutors have yet to say whether they will retry her. Facing a backlash for refusing to acquit Clancy, Desronvil has hired an attorney to represent him.The lawyer, Edward Paltzik, said in a statement on X that the \"disgusting\" defence motion was an attack on the US constitutional right to a fair trial.\"If the new normal is that jurors are going to face brutal reprisals for disagreeing with a defense attorney, you can kiss the Sixth Amendment goodbye,\" he wrote.Image source, @EdPaltzikImage caption, Desronvil (seen here in a photo provided by his lawyer) has said he had no doubt about Clancy's guiltSeveral jurors have said after the trial ended that they were ultimately divided 11-1 in favour of finding Clancy not criminally responsible.Desronvil said last week that he had no doubt about her guilt. \"As I tried to explain different possible theories during deliberation, I kept getting cut off as if I had doubts based on the evidence presented,\" he said in a statement to the BBC's US partner CBS News. The jury forewoman, a retired fifth-grade teacher, said in an interview with NBC that Desronvil had admitted he had reasonable doubt, but refused to find Clancy not guilty by reason of insanity.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-22T13:58:42.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/7999/live/356d1bb0-b683-11f1-97fb-8d66d3951490.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm4gvyw80dp2o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-22-pollock-left-money-on-table-to-stay-at-saints-hearn",
      "title": "Pollock left money on table to stay at Saints - Hearn",
      "summary": "Henry Pollock turned down rival offers worth much more than his Northampton extension, which will earn him over £400,000 a year, says adviser Eddie Hearn.",
      "body": "Henry Pollock turned down rival offers worth hundreds of thousands of pounds more than his extension to stay at boyhood club Northampton, says Eddie Hearn.Hearn, whose Matchroom promotional empire is centred on boxing, darts and snooker, has been working as Pollock's adviser since March.Pollock, 21, has agreed a new deal with Saints that extends his terms to summer 2028 and is worth more than £400,000 a year.\"There were a couple of domestic offers – a couple of Prem offers - and international offers as well,\" said Hearn of Pollock's options during his renegotiation.\"We had a very, very straight conversation. I said to him, 'be honest, do you want to go?' And he went, 'no, I really don't want to go'. And that was it.\"He knew the numbers, but ultimately he just wanted to stay at the club.\"He left a lot of money on the table to stay with Northampton. But it wasn't even a tough decision for him.\"Hearn says that some of Pollock's suitors were offering \"well in excess of seven figures\".The £1m mark was where Hearn valued Pollock's annual salary prospects in an interview with the Daily Telegraph interview in August., externalHearn acknowledges that his client's new deal \"didn't quite get to the number\" that was targeted.The headline basic figure Pollock has accepted does not account for a signing bonus and various payments he receives for off-pitch work.And Hearn says that Pollock's endorsement deals have carried him comfortably over £1m in total annual earnings.\"Everybody from major financial institutions to household brands to performance brands to nutritional brands to wellness brands… I mean, there isn't really a sector that hasn't hasn't approached us about Henry Pollock,\" said Hearn.Pollock is keen to explore the possibility of representing Great Britain in rugby sevens at the 2028 Olympics in Los Angeles.GB's qualification is far from certain after the programme went part-time and the team slipped from the top tier of the international circuit.The back row's new deal does not include the sort of dedicated time to train up for sevens that France captain Antoine Dupont took before Paris 2024, but the prospect has been raised with Saints.\"Our principal obligation is to perform for Northampton and to help them win championships,\" said Hearn.\"Part of the arrangement allows him to look at those kind of opportunities, but only with a sensible conversation with the club.\"Pollock's ability to maximise his on-pitch earnings in England is restricted by a £6.4m salary cap for Prem clubs.Each team is allowed one player whose salary is exempt from the calculations; however, players who move between top-tier teams can only qualify for such marquee status after two years with their new side.Hearn would scrap the cap entirely.\"Let the players earn,\" he said. \"All they hear about is salary caps or one star player per team - marquee names or whatever you want to call it. You don't want that.\"You want to say to a young player, 'hey, come to rugby, you can be a superstar and you can earn a fortune'.\"You could have lost Pollock and it would have been a disaster for English rugby.\"The salary cap is designed to make the Prem sustainable and competitive, with similar systems in place in most major American sports.Players such as Pollock can supplement club earnings with a central England contract, worth about £150,000 a year, or one-off Test match fees.Still, some England players, such as Jack Willis, Kyle Sinckler and Sam Simmonds, have left for France's Top 14 where a salary cap of 11m euros (£9.4m) offers scope for greater earnings.Hearn's current stable of rugby stars is only two strong. Bath and Scotland fly-half Finn Russell is similarly represented by Matchroom in off-pitch matters and advised by Hearn.However, Hearn has ambitions to become more deeply involved in the staging and promotion of the game. He is set to meet with Prem Rugby chief executive Simon Massie-Taylor in Monaco in the coming weeks to discuss ideas.\"We haven't really touched the surface in terms of what we could do in the sport,\" said Hearn.\"All I've done is done a few deals with Henry, he's come to a couple of fights and I've done a couple of Zoom calls [with journalists].\"That just shows you the buzz that can be created with a little bit of hype and perception.\"But hype and perception are nothing without substance. The sport and the brand is the substance and it's significant in rugby.\"Look at what Matchroom has done elsewhere. Boxing was dead, darts was non-existent, snooker was stuck in the air. We don't fail. Our track record is second to none.\"If we went into rugby, it would be a stroll in the park for me. It's a huge global sport steeped in history with fantastic values and tradition that just needs to be just turbo-charged.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-22T13:54:18.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/5814/live/bc1c6780-b690-11f1-b1d1-571ed4d7ff2c.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/rugby-union/articles/ckn8eq99jnevo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-22-8216-trump-tv-8217-is-just-reruns-of-the-president-on-youtub",
      "title": "&#8216;Trump TV&#8217; is just reruns of the president on YouTube",
      "summary": "The White House is now airing reruns of President Donald Trump's speeches, bill signings, and other moments on a YouTube livestream that it's calling \"Trump TV,\" as reported earlier by Time. The 24/7 station launched just days after Trump banned CNN, MS Now, and Politico from the White House.\nFollowing several teasers propping up \"Trump TV,\" Kaelan Dorr, the White House's head of digital strategy,",
      "body": "The White House is now airing reruns of President Donald Trump’s speeches, bill signings, and other moments on a YouTube livestream that it’s calling “Trump TV,” as reported earlier by Time. The 24/7 station launched just days after Trump banned CNN, MS Now, and Politico from the White House.\nFollowing several teasers propping up “Trump TV,” Kaelan Dorr, the White House’s head of digital strategy, revealed that the administration will air Trump’s “greatest hits, unfiltered” on YouTube. “The press, in some cases, reported inaccurately or not at all on the Administration’s many record breaking accomplishments on behalf of all Americans,” Dorr writes. The White House also posted a promotion for the station featuring clips from various shows, like The Office, Seinfeld, and Family Guy.\nThe livestream’s launch drew immediate criticism, with Governor Gavin Newsom calling it “state-run TV.” CNN, MS Now, and Politico also sued the Trump administration, claiming that their ban from the White House “threatens press freedom and the public’s right to independent journalism free from government interference.”\nAs reported by Time, CNN was supposed to shoot video for the press pool on Monday, but no other network took its place in support of the outlet. ABC, CBS, CNN, Fox News, and NBC later released a joint statement condemning the White House’s decision to ban a news organization because the administration “objects to its reporting.”",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "News",
        "Policy",
        "Politics",
        "Speech",
        "Streaming",
        "YouTube"
      ],
      "author": "Emma Roth",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-22T13:54:13.000Z",
