    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-22-amd-s-random-number-generator-can-t-generate-a-0",
      "title": "AMD's random number generator can't generate a 0?",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://board.flatassembler.net/topic.php?t=24261\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49798204\nPoints: 181\n# Comments: 141",
      "body": "In addition to being an assembly language programmer, I am also an electronics engineer/technician (with probably \"similar level of experience\", or even more), and therefore, with knowledge of both areas, I can think of hundreds of different ways of being able to build something that generates random numbers, and of high quality (for example, based on white noise generators, which are the thermal noise of semiconductors, try to predict this with supercomputers). Therefore, I don't think this is by far the most difficult part to do on an x86-64 processor, for the Engineering elite, present in the Intel and AMD teams, for example.\nFurthermore, I must say that I have not yet received any further feedback from AMD regarding the case. But they have already escalated my case internally with them, so I am still here waiting for a precise update from them, to share it here. And, since 9 days before posting here, until the present moment, not a single 0 came out of the 2 AMDs that I had access to!\nWith the new update I made to my application to test this, it is now also possible to benchmark the random number generator, and it is also now possible to run the test on processors that do not have the 'rdseed' instruction. As for the benchmark, it is surprising how different the results are between different processors (e.g.: a Core i5 from 2012 generates 12.6 M numbers per second, while a Core i7 7700 from 2017 generates \"only\" 750 k numbers per second, and my AMD Ryzen 7 around 2.6 M numbers per second, and the AMD generator being completely inert to clock variation, and the Core i5 from 2012 100% dependent on the clock, as it seemed to me in the tests I did). Which leads me to realize that the random number generator circuit must have large differences, and probably completely different versions/revisions, even for models from the same manufacturer.\nSo far, I also have not found decent information on how their RNG unit works.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "BruceEel",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-22T08:39:43.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://flatassembler.net/images/flatassembler.gif",
      "originalUrl": "https://board.flatassembler.net/topic.php?t=24261",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-22-apple-8217-s-first-live-music-venue-is-opening-beneath-its-u",
      "title": "Apple&#8217;s first live music venue is opening beneath its UK headquarters",
      "summary": "The venue is opening below Battersea Power Station, also well recognized for featuring on the cover of Pink Floyd’s Animals album. | Image: Apple\t\n\nApple is preparing to open a new \"state-of-the-art\" live music venue in the UK, a 600-capacity space for hosting performances, recording sessions, and video broadcasts. The venue, called Apple Music Hall, is located underneath Apple's European headquar",
      "body": "Apple is preparing to open a new “state-of-the-art” live music venue in the UK, a 600-capacity space for hosting performances, recording sessions, and video broadcasts. The venue, called Apple Music Hall, is located underneath Apple’s European headquarters in London’s iconic Battersea Power Station, and is set to open next week on September 28th. Details about the opening show have yet to be announced.\n“Apple’s deep love for music dates all the way back to the very beginning, from the launch of the iPod to the long-running iTunes Music Festival to the debut of Apple Music,” said Oliver Schusser, Apple’s vice president of Apple Music and International Content. “Today, with the opening of Apple Music Hall, we are once again taking an unprecedented step further in our commitment to artists and fans by supporting live music in ways only Apple can do.”\nThe limited capacity and layout is “designed intentionally to close the gap between intimacy and production value,” according to Apple’s announcement, with seating that feels close to the performer while providing artists with a 38-foot-wide stage that can be reconfigured to their needs. Apple Music Hall has a 48-speaker spatial sound system positioned around the audience, is hardwired for 16 or more broadcast cameras, and includes two dedicated recording and mixing studios behind the stage that allow it to operate as a full production facility.\nThe performance space is opening alongside new Apple Music radio studios in Battersea, with Apple announcing that all of its London-based hosts and shows will now broadcast from the facility. “We’re always looking for new opportunities to connect the artists and the fans because we are fans,” said Apple Music’s global creative director, Zane Lowe. “Great music and memories will be made at Apple Music Hall.”\nApple Music Hall marks the company’s return to London’s music scene, having previously run the iTunes Music Festival in England’s capital city for a decade — hosting acts like One Direction, Lady Gaga, Elton John, and Adele at intimate local venues — before its cancellation in 2017. Battersea Power Station, a decommissioned Art Deco facility that reopened in 2022, also has its own roots in music history, having appeared on the cover of Pink Floyd’s Animals album.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Apple",
        "Entertainment",
        "Music",
        "News",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Jess Weatherbed",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-22T08:35:54.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/Apple-Music-Hall-exterior.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,15.095986038394,100,69.808027923211",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/998581/apple-music-hall-london-venue-battersea",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-22-nightmare-for-taxi-drivers-as-fuel-prices-soar",
      "title": "'Nightmare' for taxi drivers as fuel prices soar",
      "summary": "Volunteer blood bike charity Freewheelers workers said they are also facing tough choices.",
      "body": "\"Obviously it's a concern because our chief business cost on a day-to-day basis is fuel. I think we're headed for pretty tough times ahead,\" Allis said.He added at least 50% of the calls he receives are requests for quotes, as customers compare his pricing with other services, such as Uber.\"We're under a continual pressure to lowball ourselves and do prices we were doing 15 years ago, even though fuel prices are at an all time high,\" he said.\"It's just a complete nightmare in that respect.\"Susannah Moffat, who runs Ledbury Road Services in Tewkesbury, Gloucestershire, said \"the last thing we want is to lose more trade\" by putting the prices up.\"The only thing we can do is keep cutting our margin, time and time again, to try and keep the price increases to a minimum,\" she said.Moffat said the petrol station was now \"relying\" on income from shop sales instead.\"It's a difficult business to be in at the moment. Seems like there's no end in sight really, but hopefully it won't be forever,\" she added.Meanwhile, volunteer blood bike charity Freewheelers, which supports the NHS by delivering lifesaving medical products, said they are \"sucking up the impact in our reserves\".The organisation covers an operational distance of more than 220,000 miles (354,055 km) per year across Bristol, Somerset, Bath and west Wiltshire. \"We've spent £17,000 on fuel per year over the last couple of years,\" said Chris MacDonald, chair of the Bristol branch.\"This current year it's heading towards £22,000, so it's a significant jump.\"The charity relies solely on public fundraising, and said if fuel prices continue to rise, they would have to reduce the number of \"low priority\" callouts.\"But we're not there yet. We can sustain the current situation for a while by digging into our reserves,\" MacDonald assured.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-22T08:33:49.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/95a8/live/978f4900-b668-11f1-b785-7d19738c6de2.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cr0qx2p587nxo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-22-pollock-signs-new-northampton-deal-in-excess-of-400k",
      "title": "Pollock signs new Northampton deal in excess of £400K",
      "summary": "Henry Pollock has brought an end to his contract stand-off with Northampton, saying it was ultimately \"a very easy decision\" to sign an extension.",
      "body": "Henry Pollock has brought an end to his contract stand-off with Northampton, saying it was ultimately \"a very easy decision\" to sign an extension with the defending Prem champions.\"I am very, very happy to sign here,\" Pollock told BBC Radio Northampton.\"I have been here my whole career so it made sense for me to stay on the journey with such a young group of players, who are so ambitious.\"It was a very easy decision in the end.\"The 21-year-old forward's new terms tie him to Northampton until the summer of 2028, raising the prospect of talks restarting in around a year's time.Pollock, whose profile has exploded over the past two seasons after appearing for England and the British and Irish Lions, had been talked up as a \"seven-figure player\" during negotiations by his advisor Eddie Hearn.However, the boxing promoter's valuation was never likely to be met at Northampton or elsewhere in the Prem, given Saints' star-studded line-up, the league's £6.4m squad salary cap and rules over players moving between clubs.The exact level of Pollock's pay increase is unclear. While some sources have valued his new contract at £400,000 a year, others involved in the deal say the figure is higher.His salary is certainly short of Scotland playmaker Finn Russell, who, as Bath's marquee player, is on the league's most lucrative package, worth about £1m a year.Pollock's pay puts him well ahead of most of his contemporaries in the Prem, however.His new deal is comfortably ahead of the average annual salary in the league (£191,000), the average earned by someone in his 20-22 age bracket (£106,000) and with his level of international experience (£224,000), as revealed by the latest league payroll data, covering the 2024-25 campaign., externalOnly 4% of Prem players that season were paid in excess of £400,000 a year.Pollock's previous contract, signed in December 2024, is understood to have been worth about £150,000.\"I don't create it, my team do,\" said Pollock of the speculation that has surrounded his future over the past seven weeks.\"It is one of those things that you have to take in your stride, but I am happy it is all done and I can focus on the rugby.\"Asked about the possibility of Saints becoming the first side since Saracens in 2019 to successfully defend their English title, Pollock was bullish.\"We have the squad depth and the belief in this group that we can do it,\" he said.\"Let's see, hopefully we can create some magic along the way. Let's write a new story.\"Pollock, who was instrumental in Northampton's run to the title last season, insisted last week that he was still open to offers, raising the possibility of a move abroad or even to another sport.However, remaining at Northampton, for whom he made his league debut in November 2023, always seemed the most likely option.After winning an age-grade world title with England's under-20s at the end of that season, Pollock returned to Franklin's Gardens and established himself as a first-team regular.His pace, energy and on-pitch persona propelled him into the public consciousness and into contention for international honours. He made his Test debut off the bench against Wales, scoring two tries, in March 2025 and was a bolter pick for the Lions squad only two months later.His try-scoring feats through Saints' run to the Champions Cup final, complete with pre-planned celebrations, earned him a spot on the eight-strong shortlist for the continental competition's player of the year.He has appeared in each of England's past 12 Tests, albeit with his only start coming in a 42-21 defeat by Ireland in February.\"Over the last few years, everyone has seen the quality he brings on the pitch and the speed of his rise through the game – but just as importantly, he's a young man of outstanding character,\" said Northampton's director of rugby Phil Dowson.\"What's most exciting is that there's still so much more to come from him. Henry is incredibly ambitious and constantly looking for ways to improve. He understands that individual success comes from contributing to a successful team, and he's already taken on leadership responsibilities within the squad despite his age.\"People sometimes underestimate how intelligent he is as a rugby player. When we sit down to review performances, he's often identified the same things we were going to discuss before we've even raised them. His understanding of the game is excellent, and that gives him a huge platform to keep progressing.\"He's become an important figure not just for Saints, but for the wider game. He inspires young supporters, brings excitement to rugby, and understands that everything off the pitch is built on performing consistently on it. We're excited to see what he can achieve over the coming years.\"",
