      "id": "bbc-2026-09-22-is-the-global-appeal-of-the-wsl-becoming-a-problem-for-homeg",
      "title": "Is the global appeal of the WSL becoming a problem for homegrown talent?",
      "summary": "The London City Lionesses raised eyebrows with no English players in their starting line-up against Manchester United, but is there a wider trend?",
      "body": "Image caption, Hannah Hampton and Lauren James are two players that have established themselves in the England starting XI under Sarina Wiegman (centre)ByJoe BradshawBBC Sport senior journalist and Alex BrothertonBBC Sport journalistWhen London City Lionesses beat Manchester United on the opening night of the 2025-26 season, it suggested times were changing in the English women's game.For some, that had become clear about an hour before kick-off in Bromley when the team news revealed a starting XI without any English players - the first time a Women's Super League side had done so on the first day of the season.Two days later it happened again, when Scotland striker Kirsty Hanson was the only British player to start for Tottenham in their win over West Ham.Both instances speak to the increasing ability of WSL clubs to attract top talents from across the globe, creating a league many now view as the world's best.But, with the Women's Champions League returning this week, is what's good for the WSL good for homegrown players, and by extension the home nations?Or is it, as The Guardian's Tom Garry puts it, \"alarming\" for the hopes of the home nations?Arsenal v HB Koge, Women's Champions LeagueTuesday, 22 September, 20:00 BSTListen to BBC Radio 5 Live commentary and follow text updates of the matches featuring English teams on the BBC Sport website as the Women's Champions League returnsThe changing make-up of the WSLTen years ago when current champions Manchester City last won the WSL title, they did so with an all-British starting line-up.Much has changed since.In 2018, the WSL went fully professional, the subsequent success of the Lionesses in the 2020s rapidly grew the game's audience and revenues have increased accordingly.As a result, clubs have expanded their scouting pool and been able to attract players from a wider variety of nationalities - with transfer fees exploding since the turn of the decade.Over recent windows, WSL clubs have recruited heavily from Germany and Sweden, while two-time Ballon d'Or winner Alexia Putellas exchanged the trophies of Barcelona for big-spending London City this summer.The proliferation of players from Japan and Australia also highlights the WSL's attractiveness for those at the top of the game as those clubs with the deepest pockets pack their squads with players from across the globe.\"Our league has grown significantly and attracted world class athletes from all over the world,\" says Birmingham City manager Amy Merricks. \"Unfortunately that blocks some of the pathways for the youngsters coming through.\"We need [this] talent in our league because our league is growing, but at the same time we have got to try and address this issue.\"Looking at the proportion of homegrown players (English, Scottish, Welsh and Northern Irish) to start 11 or more matches, half of a WSL season, the data shows a steady decline over the past three seasons to under a third.The first three weeks of 2026-27 have offered a bump on last season, but that can partly be attributed to the WSL's expansion from 12 teams to 14 teams and the greater proportion of homegrown talent in the promoted trio of Birmingham City, Crystal Palace and Charlton Athletic.It is a downward trend that is not mirrored across other major European leagues. For 2025-26, 61% of those to start half or more of Premiere Ligue matches in France were French, 63% of those in Frauen Bundesliga games were German, and 69% of Liga F players in Spain were Spanish.And, while WSL managers say they appreciate the importance of cultivating homegrown talent, the reality of the job ensures it is not their first priority.\"The club has a responsibility to raise young players, give our own talents possibilities to be in the squad and compete every week,\" says Manchester City boss Andree Jeglertz.\"But when it comes to team selection, I always need to pick the team that gives us the best chance of winning the game. If that contains eight young and promising English players or zero, I don't care. I just want to win the game.\"What is the potential impact on the national team?Image gallerySkip image gallerySlide 1 of 4, Squad graphic against Germany in Euro 2022:\nEarps, Bronze, Williamson, Bright, Daly, Walsh, Stanway, Mead, Kirby, Hemp, White, It feels like a crossroads has been reached between clubs wanting to be competitive, the WSL continuing to grow, and the need to regenerate an England squad that has not changed much over the past three major tournaments.After all, England are the two-time European champions but seven of the team that started against Spain in June also played in the Euro 2022 final against Germany - while two of the remaining starters in this year's game were on the bench four years ago.Sarina Wiegman's side were outclassed in Majorca, losing 4-0 to miss out on automatic qualification for the 2027 Women's World Cup in Brazil. They must now navigate two rounds of play-off matches, starting against Greece next month.Discussions on England's future is pertinent given the biennial Women's Under-20s World Cup is ongoing in Poland.Last Thursday, Lydia Bedford's young side were dismissed 3-0 from the last 16 by Nigeria, albeit with the caveat a number of players such as US-based Erica Meg Parkinson and Laila Harbert were not released by their clubs to play.Nevertheless, in 2022 and 2024, like the other home nations, they did not even qualify.It is a far cry from the class of 2018 where the likes of Alessia Russo, Lauren Hemp and Georgia Stanway led the Lionesses to third place in the Under-20s World Cup, setting the tone for the incredible subsequent success.Wiegman has handed debuts to 21 players since her arrival in September 2021, but only Lauren James, Hannah Hampton and Lucia Kendall have become established starters.When England kick off in Crete on Friday, 5 October (20:00 BST), it is likely to be the same faces once again.To try and facilitate the pathway to top level football for young academy players, Football Association rules require WSL sides to include eight homegrown players in their squad lists.The quota for WSL 2 clubs is higher and works on a sliding scale depending on squad size.Former England striker Ellen White told the Women's Football Weekly podcast this was insufficient, saying: \"We should be updating that quota of eight players, tweaking it to best help and complement our international structure, our homegrown talent.\"\"I would love to see them go much further - something mandated, a minimum on the pitch at any one time,\" the Guardian's Garry told BBC Women's Football Weekly. \"London City's line-up was alarming.\"It's great for the product over here, but will we see pain in 15 years' time if England are struggling to go deep in major tournaments because we're not developing players?\"Last month, the FA approved a controversial plan to allow some WSL academy sides to play in the National League from 2027-28, saying it would \"strengthen the women's football pyramid, raise standards across the league, and improve the development pathway for young players\".The FA believes this reform will address the relative lack of competitive minutes young English players receive.Chelsea have already confirmed they plan to enter an academy side in the third tier and Manchester City women's managing director Charlotte O'Neill has previously said she would want to enter a team too.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-22T06:40:41.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/2678/live/6caf7300-b34c-11f1-bc1f-3f186ca4140c.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cew9nqnz9lno?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 6,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-22-why-is-monaco-staging-a-sprint-race-f1-q-a",
      "title": "Why is Monaco staging a sprint race? F1 Q&A",
      "summary": "BBC Sport F1 correspondent Andrew Benson answers your latest questions before the Azerbaijan Grand Prix.",
      "body": "Formula 1 announced the 2027 calendar last week, with the number of sprint events increased from six to 10, and one of them Monaco.This has proved controversial with some fans.Why pick the worst track on the calendar for overtaking to have two races in one weekend, some have wondered. What's the point of having two boring races; isn't one enough?The thinking behind it, though, is less about the races than about qualifying, and how making Monaco a sprint could mix up the weekend.Monaco qualifying is renowned as one of the highlights of the season. In that case, surely two great qualifying sessions is better than one?It was also pointed out by F1 that it gives the chance of two different grid orders and therefore more variability.There is another factor to consider, too.Monaco is one of the most demanding tracks on the calendar, and it is renowned as somewhere drivers have to build up to the limit progressively so they can become confident with knowing how the car will behave when they get there.Get that wrong, push too hard too soon, and the chances of an accident are considerably higher.So asking the drivers to go straight into a qualifying after a single practice session is a really difficult challenge.It is likely to mean more mistakes, more action, more drama, and therefore more entertainment.The same goes for grand prix qualifying.Yes, by then the drivers will have done a sprint race. But there are always changes made to the cars for qualifying, so again the drivers will be going in blind to some degree.Yes, the sprint is likely to be a procession. How could it be anything else? But it adds an extra degree of jeopardy to the whole weekend.Do you think F1 might consider the F2 approach for the virtual safety car, where drivers cannot pit under a VSC but can pit under a full safety car? As a regular viewer of both series, I think this works well in F2 and is another way to solve the jeopardy a short VSC period brings to a race. - CarolineThe VSC was discussed at the so-called FIA sporting advisory committee last week, but sources say no consensus emerged there as to potential changes.It was also on the agenda for the F1 Commission meeting on Monday but there were no significant developments.Lando Norris, who lost victory in Madrid by the VSC timing, said after the race that the drivers had previously discussed with the FIA the idea that the VSC should be out for a minimum of a lap so this sort of thing could not happen.But every potential 'solution' comes with consequences. In that case, F1 might end up with a full lap of VSC just to recover a bit of debris.The VSC was introduced so that relatively small-scale incidents could be dealt with without the lengthy disruption of a full safety car.It's accepted that the safety car affords possibilities to gain and lose out, so why should the VSC not do the same? The argument is that, generally, these even out over the season, although the Norris case in Madrid had particularly dramatic consequences.Antonelli benefited from this one, for example, but has lost out in other scenarios this year. For example, when the VSC gifted Lewis Hamilton the lead in Barcelona, even if that was rendered moot by Antonelli's subsequent retirement.Why did Mercedes put George Russell on a two-stop strategy in the Spanish Grand Prix when the race has the longest pit lane of the season? George was the only driver out of the top 15 finishers on a two-stop and did this decision cost him a podium finish? - RichardMercedes were a bit stuck when the virtual safety car was deployed on lap 14 in Madrid.The team had split the drivers' tyre strategies - Kimi Antonelli took the conventional strategy and started on the mediums, Russell had gone for the hards.With overtaking so difficult in Madrid, it was always likely that one option would turn out to be sub-optimal. The timing of the VSC was very unfortunate for those who started on hard tyres because it coincided with the pit-stop window for those on mediums.Mercedes had two choices - stay out, or pit and change tyre compound, as drivers are obligated to use two compounds during a dry grand prix.Charles Leclerc, who also started on hards and was running immediately ahead of Russell, stayed out. Mercedes pitted in the hope of trying to beat Leclerc.Whichever tyre Russell chose was unlikely to make it to the end, so Mercedes knew at that point they would almost certainly have to stop again.The hard tyre was comfortably the best race tyre in Madrid, and on his mediums Russell did not have the pace to narrow the gap to Leclerc to less than it takes to do a pit stop. So there was nothing to lose by pitting again.Team boss Toto Wolff said: \"He said the tyres were not in a great shape, the mediums. We didn't think they would go to the end. (We thought) the better opportunity was not to hang out and wait for a red, but to change the tyres and get on with it.\"Russell said: \"Maybe I could have stayed out with Leclerc but I would have still finished behind him. Maybe I could have not pitted on to the hard again but everything I would have done would have brought us to the same position.\"During the Madrid race, Max Verstappen was told to hand the place back to Lewis Hamilton. Which he did. However, what would happen if Lewis had fallen maybe two or three cars further back. Does Max still have to hand back the place (and lose places to other cars) or would he just have to take a penalty? - WayneThe scenario you paint actually happened in Madrid. Verstappen also got ahead of Antonelli on the first lap, so when Red Bull told him to let Hamilton by, he also had to let the Mercedes past.Shortly after that happened, the message \"no further action\" was displayed on the timing screens and television coverage over the incident.That was because Verstappen had given the place back by then. Otherwise, he would have been penalised.So, yes, if a driver is adjudged to have committed an offence that requires giving the place back, they must do so regardless of whether there are at that point other cars between them.There are two examples of this from Fernando Alonso's career.In the 2005 Japanese Grand Prix, he passed Christian Klien's Red Bull by cutting the chicane, let him by again on the straight and then passed him into Turn One.But race director Charlie Whiting did not notice that Alonso had let Klien back past, and after some discussions with the Renault team, he demanded Alonso cede the position.That was two laps later, by which time Alonso was seven seconds up the road.At Silverstone in 2010, Alonso, now at Ferrari, was forced off the track by Robert Kubica when trying an overtaking move at Vale, and ended up passing the Renault driver off the track.Because he had been forced off, Ferrari decided not to give up the position. But Whiting told them they had to, after discussions with the team.Problem was, at exactly that point, Kubica pulled into the pits to retire, so Alonso could not give the place back - and he was given a drive-through penalty. Before he could serve it, there was then a safety-car period which closed up the field. Penalties could not be served under a safety car, so the two situations combined dropped Alonso right to the back of the field. He ended up finishing 14th, which was a costly blow to his title hopes.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-22T06:38:06.000Z",
